I'll make you a deal:  if you don't characterize my complaints and
discussion as "whining", I never will any of yours.

I'm aware of the copyright notice, of course.  It's not there to
safeguard financial interest, so I assume it's meant to control, to
some extent, the intellectual and creative work behind the name, and
that goes to prove my point that name and reputation do matter.

In practice, I think actual "ownership" of the name has zero to do
with how best to develop the game.  I'll clarify that I'm surprised
that there would be an "ownership mentality" among any stewards of the
game.  The game is "net"-hack, as is often pointed out.  

